<h1>why do some people get hired with worse credentials than me</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Because hiring managers bet on proof, not pedigree. The candidate who can show they have already done the work beats the candidate who only claims they can, even when the claim comes wrapped in a fancier degree.</p>

<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li>53% of employers have dropped degree requirements, up from 30% the year before, a 77% increase, but only 31% followed through with real change, and fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected it. <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Burning Glass Institute + HBS</a></li>
<li>Resume use fell to 67%, down from 73% in 2024, as employers hunt for signals beyond the document. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla 2025</a></li>
<li>An internship cuts the odds of underemployment by 48.5%, making lived experience the single strongest protection against the credential trap. <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">Strada + Burning Glass Institute</a></li>
<li>New-grad hiring at big tech is down more than 50% from 2019; new grads are now only ~7% of hires. The remaining slots go to people who already look like employees, not applicants. <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">SignalFire 2025</a></li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
<h2>what do hiring managers actually see when they read your resume?</h2>

<p>They see a list of institutions you paid to attend, not a record of problems you solved. The degree is a filter that passed you through, not evidence of what you can build, ship, or fix. A hiring manager at a Series B software company described it to us this way: "I get two hundred resumes from people with the same three schools. I still do not know who can write a customer email that does not make us sound like robots."</p>

<p>This is the gap that the "worse credentials" candidate slips through. They arrive with something the manager can evaluate directly: a live project, a documented decision, a piece of work with their name on it. The degree-holder arrives with a promise, and promises are cheap in a market where <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market">41.5% of recent graduates are underemployed</a>, working jobs that never required the degree in the first place.</p>

<p>The credential was designed for a different era, when information about candidates was scarce and universities were the only gatekeepers. Today the problem is not scarcity but noise. Managers need signals that cut through, and a GitHub repository with three months of commits, a marketing campaign with metrics attached, or a product spec written for a real user beats a line on a transcript every time.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>what is the hidden signaling that beats visible credentials?</h2>

<p>Hiring is prediction under uncertainty. Every manager is trying to answer one question: will this person produce more value than they cost, and will they do it fast? Credentials answer a different question entirely: did this person survive a filter? Survival is correlated with success, but correlation is not causation, and the gap is where your competition lives.</p>

<p>The hidden signals fall into three categories. First, <strong>demonstrated skill in context</strong>: work that resembles the actual job, evaluated against the actual bar. Second, <strong>social proof from inside the profession</strong>: referrals, warm introductions, evidence that people already in the field have vouched for you. Third, <strong>self-selection into hard things</strong>: the candidate who built a side project, ran a small business, or taught themselves a tool without a syllabus signals something that no GPA captures, which is the willingness to act without permission.</p>

<p>The candidate with "worse" credentials often wins because they have stacked two or three of these signals while the credential-heavy candidate has only one. A state-school graduate with a portfolio of scored simulations, a referral from a former colleague, and a blog documenting their thinking is not worse on any dimension that matters to the person making the hire: they are better informed, better tested, and lower risk.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>why does the system still pretend credentials matter?</h2>

<p>Because credentials are efficient for institutions, not because they are accurate for hiring. A university degree is a standardized filter: cheap to verify, hard to fake, legally defensible. It lets a recruiter reject two hundred applicants in an afternoon without reading a word of their actual work. The cost of this efficiency is borne by the candidate who paid for the credential and the employer who misses the self-taught operator.</p>

<p>The pretense is cracking. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers now report using skills-based hiring</a>, up from 81% the year before. The number is aspirational, not operational: many of those employers still ask for degrees on the same job posting. But the direction is clear. The credential is becoming a checkbox that some skip, not a gate that all must pass.</p>

<p>This is where the founder analogy bites. Navid calls the degree a mortgage on a depreciating asset: you borrow against your future to buy a credential that employers increasingly treat as a starting assumption, not a differentiator. The asset still has value in some paths, and honesty demands saying so. But the value is concentrated at the top of the status pyramid, not distributed across the labor market where most people actually work.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>what can you do if you are the person with better credentials on paper?</h2>

<p>Stop treating the credential as self-evident proof and start treating it as a footnote. The degree gets you into the pile, but the work gets you out of it. If you have the fancy credential and nothing else, you are in the most dangerous position: high expectations, low information, easily replaced by the next name on the list.</p>

<p>The fix is to build the same signals your competition already has. Document your work. Score it against a professional bar, not a classroom curve. Get feedback from people who do the job, not people who grade it. The <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">internship that cuts underemployment by 48.5%</a> works because it converts credential into proof, and you can manufacture the same conversion without waiting for a formal program.</p>

<p>The deeper shift is psychological. The credential system trains you to seek permission: take this course, pass this test, receive this approval. The hiring market rewards people who act without it. The candidate with worse credentials who got hired ahead of you did not cheat the system: they recognized that the system they were trained to please was not the system that actually decides.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>how does zero change the signal structure?</h2>

<p>zero inverts the stack. Instead of degree first, proof first. You work through company-shaped scenarios, submit against a professional bar, receive per-submission feedback, and build a scored record that recruiters can search and filter. The credential becomes an ornament, and the work becomes the argument.</p>

<p>This is the structural replacement for the filter OS that Navid describes: a time-based system where you progress by waiting and not failing, replaced by a proof-based system where you progress by doing and being measured. The candidate with worse credentials wins today because they accidentally found proof, and zero makes proof the default path rather than the lucky exception.</p>

<p>The recruiter side completes the loop. Employers pay for access to scored talent, not for access to a database of degrees. The matching is specific: this person scored above threshold on this scenario, which correlates with this role. It is the Google ads algorithm for people, but the targeting is based on demonstrated work rather than inferred potential.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
<h2>frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>should I finish my degree if I already have good proof of work?</h3>
<p>It depends on your field and your risk tolerance. In some roles, particularly regulated ones, the degree remains a hard gate. In most technology, creative, and business roles, proof of work is already the stronger signal. The honest frame: the degree is insurance, not investment. Buy it if you need the safety net, but do not pretend it replaces the work.</p>

<h3>how do I show proof of work if my current job does not let me share it?</h3>
<p>Build parallel work. Run the same analysis on public data, design the same feature for an open-source project, write the same strategy memo for a company you admire and publish it as a case study. The constraint is rarely confidentiality. It is usually the discomfort of working in public without a grade to validate you.</p>

<h3>do referrals still matter more than anything else?</h3>
<p>Referrals are powerful because they transfer trust, not because they replace skill. A warm introduction gets you the interview, but scored work is what turns it into an offer. The ideal candidate has both. The mistake is treating the referral as sufficient and neglecting the proof that closes the deal.</p>

<h3>why do some companies still insist on elite degrees?</h3>
<p>Status certainty. Hiring from Harvard or IIT is a defensible decision even if the candidate fails, because the blame attaches to the institution, not the hiring manager. This is rational cowardice, not rational selection. The companies that break this pattern gain access to talent their competitors miss, which is why the shift is accelerating even at firms that once treated pedigree as mandatory.</p>

<h3>is this just a tech industry phenomenon?</h3>
<p>It started there, but it is spreading. Healthcare, finance, logistics, and professional services are all seeing the same pressure: AI handles the routine, humans handle the judgment, and judgment is hard to certify with a transcript. The frontier moves first. The rest follows as the economics become undeniable.</p>
</section>

